    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Microtubules are key components of the cytoskeleton and facilitate a number of cellular processes such as intracellular transport, structural support, and mitosis. In dividing animal cells, microtubules are organized around the centrosome. Upon differentiation in some tissues such as epithelia and muscle, microtubules are reorganized around a different site including the apical membrane or nuclear envelope, respectively. These non-centrosomal microtubule organizing centers (ncMTOCs) are found in plant and most differentiated animal cell types, yet little is understood about how cells establish an ncMTOC and the importance of non-centrosomal microtubules for cell function. C. elegans is an ideal system to study ncMTOCs, because in all differentiated cells thus far investigated, the centrosome is inactivated and an ncMTOC is designated. We sought to find a tissue that displays a marked phenotype when microtubules are disrupted in order to study general mechanisms of microtubule organization and function. We began by investigating microtubule function in epithelial tissue by overexpressing the microtubule depolymerizing enzyme SPAS-1 in the embryonic intestinal epithelium. SPAS-1 overexpression resulted in microtubule degradation and slowed total organismal growth. However, SPAS-1 expression and/or perturbation of microtubules was not consistent in this tissue. We therefore also characterized microtubule organization in C. elegans body wall muscle (BWM). Muscle cell microtubules are distinctly organized around another ncMTOC, the nuclear envelope, in most species. However, visualizing fluorescently tagged microtubule-associated proteins revealed two distinct populations of BWM microtubules in larval and adult worms. These results demonstrate that C. elegans BWM cells possess a unique microtubule organization. Furthermore, preliminary data suggest that BWM and sex muscle microtubules can be perturbed by SPAS-1 overexpression. Our combined results indicate that BWM is a strong candidate for future microtubule studies.",
